      <h1>Salary discussions without quoting a number first</h1>
      <p class="blog-read">1 min read</p>
      <div class="post-body"><p>Salary discussions make candidates nervous, and nervous candidates often name a figure first and anchor the conversation low. A better approach is to let the employer share the range first, while you prepare your response for either direction. Preparation, not boldness, is what protects you in this conversation.</p><h3>Handling the discussion calmly</h3><ul><li>Research typical pay for the role and city beforehand, so you recognise a fair range when you hear one.</li><li>If asked for expectations early, say you would like to understand the full role first, and ask about their range.</li><li>When a range is offered, pause before answering. A short silence reads as thoughtfulness, not weakness.</li><li>Consider the whole offer: shift, location, growth, and probation terms, not only the headline figure.</li></ul><p>Never accept on the spot if you feel pressured. Asking for a day to consider is normal, and reasonable employers expect it.</p></div>
